#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cacad9b6-d96e-5ed3-8941-1b425e4561b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466097} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca4bf25-4a12-5fe7-b403-40cd2d1ae306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cacad9b6-d96e-5ed3-8941-1b425e4561b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466097} :answer-field/id #uuid "52118cf3-1913-5b6f-a6a4-3a44d5057427" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16526fc2-0575-5da4-9643-0dde15f08f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466099} {:answer/id #uuid "1b41930f-abcc-5604-be55-86986ebf53ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16526fc2-0575-5da4-9643-0dde15f08f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466099} :answer-field/id #uuid "516c2af8-6bdf-5d44-a25e-66e659569bb0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1fdffc-3d4c-5f3c-b3fd-1c4e03051b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466101} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d8859c-a702-5bff-9977-f3bbc69941ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8d8859c-a702-5bff-9977-f3bbc69941ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466102} :answer-field/id #uuid "a659403e-5966-58dd-8b52-749c8e0a467c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81f632f0-dedf-5479-9362-313bdb74c812" :question/math-academy-id "q-333693" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$\n- **II.** $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23973e17-4d84-522b-8415-f15d8d6cd355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305490} {:answer/id #uuid "77acd52d-6e2b-5ae0-8067-e20d4ca97301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306288} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2323cf-e7c5-59dc-ab1f-517b7cc509f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306356} {:answer/id #uuid "85bae4c8-8a16-5414-8609-ee2e3824871a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306428} {:answer/id #uuid "91d3d298-b109-57e9-a129-668ed74e99ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2323cf-e7c5-59dc-ab1f-517b7cc509f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306356} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df9b1f6-ae90-53f3-b7f1-0e157eeb1df7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcb96b5a-0e95-52c4-ac1f-597bc14204c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334645" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.16,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.16)=0.40.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.04,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.04)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.16$ to $0.04.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.16}{0.04}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.40$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.40}{0.005}=80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.161… \\\\ p & ≈2.161….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a98936b-f6f9-53c3-aefc-f49cb4b5c873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.865" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396389} {:answer/id #uuid "55c03e15-db65-5bda-97c7-68787c5792a7" :answer/typ